Consider the scalar Riccati differential equation
\[ \dot{x} \;=\; A_2(t)\,x^2 \;+\; A_1(t)\,x \;+\; A_0(t), \]
where $A_0, A_1, A_2 : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ are continuous and $T$-periodic functions for some fixed $T>0$. A $T$-periodic solution is a solution $x(t)$ defined for all $t\in\mathbb{R}$ satisfying $x(t+T)=x(t)$. A limit cycle is an isolated $T$-periodic solution (isolated in the set of all $T$-periodic solutions, with the topology of uniform convergence on $[0,T]$).

It is known that such an equation either possesses a continuum of $T$-periodic solutions, or has at most two $T$-periodic solutions (hence at most two limit cycles, with possible counts $0$, $1$, or $2$).

Problem. Provide effective criteria, expressed directly in terms of the coefficient functions $A_0(t), A_1(t), A_2(t)$, that decide which of the following four mutually exclusive cases occurs:
(i) the equation admits a continuum of $T$-periodic solutions;
(ii) the equation has exactly $2$ limit cycles;
(iii) the equation has exactly $1$ limit cycle;
(iv) the equation has $0$ limit cycles.

By "effective" one means a criterion that can be checked by a finite procedure or a closed-form computation involving $A_0, A_1, A_2$ and their integrals/averages, rather than requiring knowledge of the explicit solutions or of the Poincaré (return) map.

